Chronic Venous Insufficiency
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Localized Fluid Shift
Chronic dependent pitting edema, Hemosiderin staining, Lipodermatosclerosis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-011
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe, painful lower limb swelling and weeping skin ulceration, requiring urgent after-hours evaluation for impending cellulitis or deep vein thrombosis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a sudden worsening of chronic leg swelling complicated by new, intense, throbbing pain and weeping skin. The assessment indicated an urgent need to evaluate for secondary skin infection or acute deep venous occlusion, which could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period without risking rapid soft tissue deterioration.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Severe aching, heavy legs with weeping, itchy skin. Patient states, My legs feel like lead weights, and the skin is so tight and itchy I can't sleep.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have evolved over several years, initially presenting as chronic bilateral pitting lower limb edema that worsens progressively throughout the day and partially improves with elevation. Over the past 48 hours, the patient reports a sudden escalation in the heavy, aching, and throbbing sensation in both calves. There is marked venous claudication upon brief ambulation. The patient notes an exacerbation of venous eczema, presenting as intensely pruritic, erythematous, scaling skin that has begun to weep serous fluid today, raising concern for infection. A history of a prior deep vein thrombosis is noted.
Functional Status: Functionally impaired; currently mobilizing only for essential needs due to pain and profound leg heaviness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of developing chronic, non-healing venous stasis ulcers; lives alone with limited assistance for daily wound care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 92.0 Kg
General: Appears uncomfortable, frequently rubbing the affected lower limbs, seated with legs in a dependent position.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Normal heart sounds. Peripheral pulses (dorsalis pedis, posterior tibial) are palpable bilaterally despite swelling. Respiratory: Lungs clear. Integumentary/Vascular: The diagnostic triad is clinically profound: examination reveals severe chronic dependent pitting edema (3+) extending to the proximal calves. The skin exhibits distinct hemosiderin staining, appearing as dense hyperpigmentation around the medial malleoli. Lipodermatosclerosis is evident, causing induration and a rigid champagne bottle appearance of the lower legs. Erythematous, scaling patches of venous eczema are present, weeping clear serous fluid but lacking frankly purulent discharge, localized heat, or ascending lymphangitis.
Environmental Observations: Patient sleeps in a standard bed without foot elevation; trip hazards present due to clustered furniture.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient has not been utilizing prescribed compression stockings.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Venous Insufficiency - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral presentation, absence of systemic fever, and lack of unilateral, rapidly spreading, hot, tender erythema (the erythema is scaling and eczematous).

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical bilateral swelling without acute, unilateral calf tenderness or a newly palpable venous cord, though the risk remains high given the history.

Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of dyspnea, orthopnea, elevated jugular venous pressure, or pulmonary crackles.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The clinical presentation is dominated by acute-on-chronic localized fluid shifts secondary to severe valvular incompetence, manifesting as weeping venous eczema and intense leg heaviness. The sudden escalation of weeping skin and severe pruritus necessitated an urgent after-hours medical evaluation to definitively differentiate a benign eczematous flare from early bilateral cellulitis or an acute thrombotic event. Delaying this assessment would have risked rapid progression to an intractable, infected venous ulcer. The immediate physical examination confirmed the absence of acute infection, allowing for the safe initiation of aggressive topical steroid therapy, barrier protection, and limb elevation protocols. The expected investigation results include superficial or deep venous reflux greater than 0.5 seconds on duplex ultrasonography that would assist in confirming the diagnosis at a later vascular surgical review.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a moderate-potency topical corticosteroid (Betamethasone valerate 0.1% ointment) applied sparingly to eczematous areas to halt weeping and pruritus. Prescribed a thick emollient for surrounding dry skin. Advised strict limb elevation.
Positive Behavioral Change: To dramatically reduce the heavy, throbbing pain, implement a strict schedule of elevating your legs above the level of your heart for 30 minutes, three times a day. Use specific pillows designated for this task. As the swelling visibly decreases and the pain subsides, use that physical relief as the direct reward to maintain this daily habit, actively taking control of your fluid buildup.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to community nursing for regular lower limb wound assessment and application of compression bandaging once weeping resolves.
DME/Safety (Optional): Leg elevation wedge pillow for the bed.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in one week to assess skin integrity and arrange formal vascular ultrasound.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Seek immediate medical attention if one leg suddenly becomes significantly more swollen than the other, if the skin becomes hot to the touch, rapidly red, and excessively painful, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis reflects advanced venous disease; without strict adherence to compression and elevation, chronic wounds will inevitably form.
Clinical Course: The valves in the leg veins are failing, causing fluid to pool locally. Topical treatments will manage the current rash, but mechanical elevation and compression are the only long-term solutions.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not scratch the weeping areas, as this will introduce bacteria and cause a severe infection requiring intravenous antibiotics.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The steroid ointment directly reduces the inflammation and weeping of the skin, while elevating your legs uses gravity to manually drain the pooled fluid back toward your heart.
Actionable Steps: Elevate legs above the heart for 30 minutes, three times daily. Apply ointment only to the red, itchy patches.

